{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Send for yourself men</b> — according to your own judgment; I am not commanding you. If you wish, send. For Israel came and said, \"Let us send men ahead of us,\" as it is stated: \"And you all drew near to me\" (Deuteronomy 1:22), and Moses consulted the Shekhinah. He said: I had already told them that it is good, as it is stated: \"I will bring you up out of the affliction of Egypt to a good land\" (Exodus 3:17) — by their lives, I give them an opening to stumble through the matter of the spies, so that they shall not inherit it. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 13:2) from aggadic teachings.\n\nNow there is room to question this: if so, Moses himself sinned in the matter, for it is stated, \"And the thing was good in my eyes\" (Deuteronomy 1:23). And why did he say to them concerning the land, \"Whether it is good or bad\" (Numbers 13:19), after it had already been told to him from the outset that it is good and spacious? Furthermore, what did the spies do wrong, since Moses said to them, \"And you shall see the land, what it is, and the people who dwell upon it — whether they are strong or weak, whether they are few or many\" (Numbers 13:18), and he said to them concerning the cities, \"Whether in camps or in fortresses\" (Numbers 13:19)? In any case they needed to answer him regarding what he had commanded them, so what was their transgression and what was their sin when they said to him, \"However, the people is mighty and the cities are fortified, very great\" (Numbers 13:28)? Did he send them on condition that they testify falsely to him?! And do not suppose that their transgression was only in saying \"a land that devours its inhabitants\" (Numbers 13:32), for before they said this to them, Caleb was already contending with them — and thus it is written, \"Our brothers melted our hearts, saying, the people is greater and taller than we\" (Deuteronomy 1:28), and here it is written, \"Our wives and our children shall be plunder\" (Numbers 14:3). Now Moses our Master said these very things to their sons, and he went to great lengths regarding the strength of the people, the fortification of their cities, and the might of the giants — far more than what the spies had said to their fathers — as it is written: \"Hear, O Israel: you pass over the Jordan this day to possess nations greater and mightier than yourself, cities great and fortified up to heaven, a people great and tall, the children of the giants, whom you know and of whom you have heard: who can stand before the children of the giant?\" (Deuteronomy 9:1–2). If the transgression of the spies and their sin lay in this, why would he dissuade the hearts of their sons just as the spies dissuaded the hearts of their fathers?\n\nFurthermore, what was Moses our Master's reasoning in this mission? If the land is good and the people are weak — well and good; but if it is bad or the people are strong — does he think he would send them back to Egypt?!\n\nRather, the resolution of the matter is this: Israel spoke in the manner of all who come to wage war in a foreign land, who send men ahead of them to know the roads and the approaches to the cities, and upon their return the scouts go at the head of the army to guide them along the roads, as in the matter stated: \"Show us, we pray, the entrance to the city\" (Judges 1:24), and to give them counsel as to which city they should fight first and from which side it would be easiest to conquer the land. And so they said explicitly: \"And they shall bring us back word concerning the way by which we must go up, and the cities\" etc. (Deuteronomy 1:22) — that is, the cities to which we shall come first, and from there we shall come into the entire land. This is a sound strategy for all conquerors of lands, and Moses himself did likewise, as it is stated: \"And Moses sent to spy out Jazer\" (Numbers 21:32), and so did Joshua son of Nun: \"two men, spies\" (Joshua 2:1).\n\nTherefore the thing was good in Moses' eyes, for Scripture does not rely on miracle in all its deeds, but it commands those who wage war to arm themselves and take precautions and lay ambushes, as Scripture states regarding the battle of Ai (ibid. 8:2), which was by the word of HaShem, and in many places. So Moses consulted the Shekhinah, and HaShem gave him permission and said to him: \"Send for yourself men and let them scout the land of Canaan\" — and they shall know it and report to you, and according to their word you shall take counsel concerning the conquest.\n\nNow Moses said to them, \"Go up this way in the Negev\" (Numbers 13:17), and his reason is: go up this way through the Negev, so that they may know the people dwelling in the land of the Negev, on the side from which Israel was situated — whether they are strong, and they will need in their affairs to be very careful and to arm themselves; and likewise the cities, whether they are fortified, so that they may take refuge in them and it will be necessary to build siege-works and ramps, or to come from another side. And he said further that they should know regarding the land itself whether it is good or bad, for if it is bad they should conquer first from other regions, for they were to scout the hill country of the Amorites from the side of Hebron, for even Joshua did not conquer all of it. This is the meaning of \"and what the land is that he dwells in\" (Numbers 13:19) — concerning the people dwelling in the Negev.\n\nAnd it is possible that Moses, because he knew that it is fat and good, as he had been told, \"to a good and spacious land\" etc. (Exodus 3:8), therefore told them to pay attention to know this, so that they might report it to the people, and they would rejoice and renew their strength to go up there with joy. Therefore he said to them, \"And be strong and take of the fruit of the land\" (Numbers 13:20), so that they would see with their own eyes the praise of the land. And it is well known that Egypt is not very far from Hebron — only a journey of some seven days — and the land of Canaan touches its border near Egypt, and it is impossible that the inhabitants of Egypt would not know the nature of the land of Canaan, whether it is good or bad. But Moses' intention was to know the way by which they would go up and the cities they would conquer first, as I have explained.\n\nNow it is true that Israel were slaves in Egypt, subjected to crushing labor; they did not know and did not understand. Therefore Moses wanted them to report all matters of the land to gladden them with its qualities, for he knew this was present among them.\n\nAnd what seems right in my eyes according to the language of Scripture is that Moses did not consult the Shekhinah; rather, the reason for <b>send for yourself</b> is that they had agreed to send spies, and the custom was to send two men as spies secretly, and to send some of their number, and HaShem — who knows the future — commanded him that he should send one man, one man from every tribe of Israel, and that they should be the princes among them, for HaShem desired that all the great men share equally in the matter — perhaps they would remember and return to HaShem; and if not, that the decree be equal upon all the people. This is the meaning of \"by the word of HaShem\" (Numbers 13:3) — that they were princes and heads of the children of Israel by the command of HaShem.\n\nAnd what seems right to me according to the plain sense of Scripture is that HaShem did not mention to Moses the request that they made to send spies, nor Moses' agreement with them. For had that been so, Scripture would have recounted here, \"And the children of Israel drew near to Moses and said, Let us send men ahead of us\" etc., \"and the thing was good in the eyes of Moses,\" and afterward it would have written, \"And HaShem spoke to Moses, saying: Send for yourself men, one man for each tribe, as they spoke to you\" etc. Rather, the matter was thus: Israel asked for the mission and the thing was good in Moses' eyes, and afterward the divine word came to Moses as with all other divine words, and He said to him simply, \"Send for yourself men.\" And this is the meaning of \"and they shall scout the land of Canaan which I am giving to the children of Israel\" — for He is speaking of a new matter that had not been recounted at all.\n\nAll of this was because HaShem desired, for the sake of His justice, that the mission be by His command and that it involve all their tribes and their great men, so that they might be vindicated. And so it further appears that they asked of Moses, \"Let us send men ahead of us and let them search out the land for us\" (Deuteronomy 1:22), and this is a searching out of the roads and of the matter of conquest, in the sense of \"searching out food\" (Job 39:29). And this is the meaning of \"ahead of us\" — that they would go after them on their way, in the language of \"and the Ark of the Covenant of HaShem journeyed ahead of them\" (Numbers 10:33). But HaShem commanded <b>and they shall scout the land of Canaan</b>, and this is in the sense of one who chooses among those who come to purchase something, in the language of \"apart from the men of the scouts and the merchants\" (2 Chronicles 9:14), and so \"to the land which I searched out for them\" (Ezekiel 20:6), and so \"to scout out a resting-place for them\" (Numbers 10:33).\n\nTherefore Moses commanded them to specify \"whether it is good or bad\" etc., \"whether it is fat or lean\" etc. — all to gladden them, for it is the ornament of all lands, and they would go up into it with great desire. Now here the matter is stated simply, as it was; but in Mishneh Torah Moses recounted all these things from their beginning, to declare to them their transgression — for they sinned in that they themselves sought and asked. According to the opinion of our Sages, they sinned in saying \"Let us send men ahead of us,\" because they witnessed the salvation of HaShem performed for them constantly, and they should have followed the Cloud to wherever the direction was to go. Moses accepted this from them to fulfill their desire, and the meaning of \"and the thing was good in my eyes\" (Deuteronomy 1:23) is: I accepted your harm and instructed that it be done. And HaShem commanded him to send one man, one man for his father's tribe etc., as in the matter stated regarding Samuel: \"Listen to the voice of the people in all that they say to you, for it is not you they have rejected, but Me they have rejected from reigning over them\" (1 Samuel 8:7).\n\nNow these men were not designated by name by the word of HaShem, as was the case with those counted (above 1:5–15) and with those who divided the land (below 34:19–28), for a command of HaShem brings no stumbling-block to those who fulfill it, and one who keeps a commandment shall know no evil thing. Rather, He, blessed be He, commanded Moses, \"One man for his father's tribe you shall send\" etc., and that they should be princes; and Moses, by his own judgment, selected these men and sent them, and they repaid their own souls with evil."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:05:28.679630736Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}